Subject: mtd: rawnand: meson: refuse writes when the ECC cannot satisfy the chip, and log its full ID

Follows the first two field reports of this port on Meson8b (S805)
silicon. Both boards probed: the clocks, the pinmux, RESET and READ ID all
came out right. What went wrong on the second one is the reason for this
patch.

That board, a Xunlei WS1508, carries a Hynix H27UBG8T2C -- 8KiB pages --
which nand_base decoded as 4KiB pages with 128 bytes of OOB (fixed by full
ID in the previous patch). With that geometry the driver attached, and its
owner booted with meson_nand.allow_write=1 and ran ubiformat. Every row
address the core sent then meant something else to the chip: erase block E
became physical block 2E, and from the middle of the core's idea of the
device upwards the rows ran past the end of the chip. The address guard
from the write-protect patch could not object, because it reasons in the
same wrong geometry; and nothing else stopped it, because allow_write is
the user's switch and the user had thrown it.

The one visible symptom was a warning nand_base already prints:

  the ECC used on your system (8b/1024B) is too weak compared to the one
  required by the NAND chip (32b/1024B)

That is not a coincidence of this one part. A die whose page is
under-reported has its OOB under-reported with it, so the strongest BCH
that fits the reported OOB falls short of the requirement the same ID
bytes encode. On this experimental family, take that warning as the
refusal it should have been: if nand_ecc_is_strong_enough() fails, clear
MTD_WRITEABLE regardless of allow_write, and say why. Two separate reasons
make it correct even when the geometry is right -- data written with too
weak an ECC does not survive on the part -- and neither reason is one a
module parameter should be able to wave through.

Only SoCs marked .experimental are affected, as with the rest of the
guard; GXL and AXG behave exactly as before.

Also print the full ID on the attach report line and in the "new OOB"
refusal. nand_base logs only the manufacturer and device bytes, and on
Hynix MLC parts the geometry comes from bytes 3 to 5: the WS1508 report
could only be traced to a part number by reproducing the decode from the
vendor's ID table, because the two bytes in the log fit three different
chips.

And replace the "not validated on silicon" note on meson8_data with what
is now known: the probe path is, the data path is still not.

diff --git a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c
index dc79c79..3d90ec2 100644
--- a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c
@@ -1725,8 +1725,8 @@ static int meson_nand_attach_chip(struct nand_chip *nand)
 	 */
 	if (nfc->data->new_oob_over_4k && mtd->writesize > 4096) {
 		dev_err(nfc->dev,
-			"%u byte page: the vendor wrote this chip in the M8 \"new OOB\" layout, which this driver does not implement\n",
-			mtd->writesize);
+			"%u byte page (id %*ph): the vendor wrote this chip in the M8 \"new OOB\" layout, which this driver does not implement\n",
+			mtd->writesize, nand->id.len, nand->id.data);
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 	}
 
@@ -1806,13 +1806,19 @@ static int meson_nand_attach_chip(struct nand_chip *nand)
 	 * Everything a first bring-up on new silicon needs in order to decide
 	 * whether the clock, the pinmux and the ECC choice came out right, on
 	 * one line, so it can be read out of a boot log instead of a debugger.
+	 *
+	 * The full ID belongs on it too.  nand_base prints only the first two
+	 * bytes, and on Hynix MLC parts the geometry is decoded from bytes 3-5:
+	 * the first field report from a Meson8b board came from a die whose
+	 * page size that decode gets wrong by a factor of two, and the two
+	 * bytes in the log were not enough to tell which part it was.
 	 */
 	dev_info(nfc->dev,
-		 "page %u oob %u erase %u ecc %d/%d bch %u scramble %d clk %lu Hz\n",
+		 "page %u oob %u erase %u ecc %d/%d bch %u scramble %d clk %lu Hz id %*ph\n",
 		 mtd->writesize, mtd->oobsize, mtd->erasesize,
 		 nand->ecc.size, nand->ecc.strength, meson_chip->bch_mode,
 		 !!(nand->options & NAND_NEED_SCRAMBLING),
-		 clk_get_rate(nfc->nand_clk));
+		 clk_get_rate(nfc->nand_clk), nand->id.len, nand->id.data);
 
 	return ret;
 }
@@ -1934,11 +1940,41 @@ meson_nfc_nand_chip_init(struct device *dev,
 		meson_chip->orig_block_markbad = mtd->_block_markbad;
 		mtd->_block_markbad = meson_nfc_block_markbad_guard;
 
-		if (!meson_nfc_allow_write) {
+		/*
+		 * An ECC weaker than the chip asks for is a reason to refuse
+		 * writes that allow_write must not be able to override, for
+		 * two reasons.  The obvious one: what this driver wrote would
+		 * not survive on the part.  The less obvious one is that the
+		 * mismatch is often the only visible symptom of a mis-decoded
+		 * ID.  A die whose page size nand_base under-reports also has
+		 * its OOB under-reported, so the strongest BCH that fits the
+		 * reported OOB falls short of the requirement the same ID
+		 * bytes encode -- and every row address this driver then sends
+		 * lands on a different physical page than it believes,
+		 * including rows past the end of the chip, which on a part
+		 * that ignores the excess address bits come round to the
+		 * vendor boot region.  The address guard reasons in the same
+		 * wrong geometry and cannot catch that.  This is not
+		 * hypothetical: it is what the first field report from a
+		 * Meson8b board (a Hynix H27UBG8T2C read as 4KiB pages instead
+		 * of 8KiB) ran into with allow_write=1.
+		 */
+		if (!nand_ecc_is_strong_enough(&nand->base)) {
+			const struct nand_ecc_props *req =
+				nanddev_get_ecc_requirements(&nand->base);
+
+			/* partitions inherit this, so it covers them too */
+			mtd->flags &= ~MTD_WRITEABLE;
+			dev_warn(dev,
+				 "%s: read-only whatever meson_nand.allow_write says: the chip requires %ub/%uB ECC but runs %db/%dB in %u OOB bytes, which can also mean the geometry decoded from id %*ph is wrong\n",
+				 mtd->name, req->strength, req->step_size,
+				 nand->ecc.strength, nand->ecc.size,
+				 mtd->oobsize, nand->id.len, nand->id.data);
+		} else if (!meson_nfc_allow_write) {
 			/* partitions inherit this, so it covers them too */
 			mtd->flags &= ~MTD_WRITEABLE;
 			dev_warn(dev,
-				 "%s: read-only, this SoC's NAND port has never been validated on real silicon; boot with meson_nand.allow_write=1 to override\n",
+				 "%s: read-only, this SoC's NAND port has not been validated for writing on real silicon; boot with meson_nand.allow_write=1 to override\n",
 				 mtd->name);
 		}
 
@@ -2047,10 +2083,16 @@ static const struct meson_nfc_data meson_axg_data = {
  * SD_EMMC_C divider, a BCH60 boot mode instead of BCH8, an unconditional
  * scrambler, and a "new OOB" page layout this driver does not implement.
  *
- * NOT VALIDATED ON SILICON.  The only reported bring-up on this family was an
- * out-of-tree Meson8m2 attempt in 2019 that never landed, and no Meson8b board
- * has ever had an NFC node.  Hence .experimental, which the write protection
- * in the following patch keys off.
+ * PROBE VALIDATED ON SILICON, DATA PATH NOT.  Two Meson8b (S805) boards have
+ * run this: on both the controller clocks, the pinmux takes, and RESET and
+ * READ ID come back right, and on one of them erase and program completed at
+ * the command level as well.  Neither got further, for reasons that are not
+ * this driver's: one carried a 16KiB-page part (beyond NFC_CMD_RAW_LEN and in
+ * the "new OOB" layout), the other an 8KiB-page part that nand_base
+ * mis-decoded as 4KiB.  Nobody has yet checked a page this driver reads
+ * against what the vendor stack reads from the same page, so the ECC,
+ * scrambler and OOB handling remain unverified -- hence .experimental, which
+ * the write protection in the following patch keys off.
  *
  * There is deliberately no per-SoC NFC round-trip delay here.  The obvious
  * candidate, NAND_CYCLE_DELAY, exists three times in Amlogic's own tree with
